export const V1={title:'The veterinary team, the event and ethical, legal and safety practice',hook:'A veterinary clinic may see a puppy for vaccines, a cat with kidney disease, a hamster that stopped eating and a horse with colic—all in one day. Veterinarians, technicians and assistants care for patients who cannot describe their symptoms, while protecting public health from diseases that spread between animals and people. This lesson introduces the event, the team and the rules that keep animals, clients and staff safe.',objectives:['Describe the Veterinary Science event, its test plan and its seven skills.','Compare the roles and education of veterinarians, veterinary technicians and assistants.','Apply veterinary ethics, the veterinarian–client–patient relationship and animal welfare principles.','Identify workplace hazards in veterinary practice and how to prevent injuries.'],sections:[
['The event at a glance',[
'Veterinary Science has two rounds. **Round One** is a **50-question test in 60 minutes** covering **large and small animals**: anatomy and physiology **24%**, diseases and disease prevention **14%**, emergency care and first aid **12%**, principles of surgery **12%**, basic nutrients **10%**, clinical practice **10%**, animal behavior **6%**, zoonoses **6%** and ethical, legal and safety **6%**. References are Lawhead and Baker’s *Introduction to Veterinary Science*, the American Red Cross Pet First Aid app and the AKC and CFA breed pages.',
'**Round Two** skills: **I** identify instruments and equipment (15 minutes; name, spelling and purpose), **II** identify companion animal breeds and species (8 minutes; name and spelling), **III** preparation of the operative site (7), **IV** simple fecal flotation (5), **V** lifting and restraining a dog (4), **VI** wrapping a surgical pack (3) and **VII** preparing a feline to obtain a temperature (6). Skills I and II may be given with the test. **Stuffed animals** are used—never live animals. Bring photo ID and **non-latex exam gloves**. You need **70%** on the skills (excluding the test and the two identification skills); ties go to the higher test score.',
{t:'table',caption:'Written test plan',head:['Area','Weight'],rows:[['Anatomy and physiology','24%'],['Diseases and disease prevention','14%'],['Emergency care and first aid','12%'],['Principles of surgery','12%'],['Basic nutrients','10%'],['Clinical practice','10%'],['Animal behavior','6%'],['Zoonoses','6%'],['Ethical, legal and safety','6%']]}]],
['The veterinary team',[
'**Veterinarians** earn a **Doctor of Veterinary Medicine (DVM or VMD)** after four years of veterinary school, usually following a bachelor’s degree, and pass the North American Veterinary Licensing Examination and state requirements. They diagnose, prescribe, perform surgery and make treatment decisions. Many specialize in surgery, internal medicine, dentistry, emergency and critical care, food-animal medicine, public health or research.',
'**Credentialed veterinary technicians** (CVT, LVT or RVT, depending on the state) complete an accredited two- or four-year program and pass the **Veterinary Technician National Examination (VTNE)**. They are similar to nurses: they monitor anesthesia, place catheters, take radiographs, run lab tests, give medications as directed and educate clients. **Veterinary assistants** help with restraint, feeding, cleaning and kennel care. Receptionists and practice managers keep the clinic running.',
sort('Who usually does it?',['Veterinarian','Credentialed technician','Veterinary assistant'],[['Diagnoses and prescribes',0],['Performs surgery',0],['Monitors anesthesia',1],['Takes radiographs and runs lab tests',1],['Cleans kennels and feeds patients',2],['Helps restrain animals',2]])]],
['Ethics and the law',[
'The **AVMA Principles of Veterinary Medical Ethics** call on veterinarians to put animal welfare first, act honestly, keep client information confidential and refer cases beyond their abilities. A valid **veterinarian–client–patient relationship (VCPR)** is required before a veterinarian diagnoses, treats or prescribes: the veterinarian takes responsibility for clinical judgments, the client agrees to follow instructions and the veterinarian has examined the animal recently enough to know its condition. **Informed consent** forms are signed before surgery and anesthesia.',
'**Animal welfare** is often described with the **Five Freedoms**: freedom from hunger and thirst; from discomfort; from pain, injury and disease; to express normal behavior; and from fear and distress. Laws regulate controlled drugs (DEA registration and logs for drugs such as opioids and ketamine), reporting of certain diseases (such as rabies) to public health authorities, medical records, and food-animal drug **withdrawal times** so drug residues do not enter milk or meat. **Euthanasia** decisions require compassion, consent and correct procedures.',
flip('Ethics and law terms',[['VCPR','Veterinarian–client–patient relationship required before treatment'],['Informed consent','Owner approves after learning risks and costs'],['Five Freedoms','Basic welfare standards for animals'],['Withdrawal time','Wait after drug use before milk or meat enters the food supply'],['Reportable disease','Must be reported to authorities, such as rabies'],['Confidentiality','Client information is private']])]],
['Safety in the veterinary workplace',[
'Common injuries in veterinary practice are **bites and scratches**, **back injuries** from lifting, **needlesticks**, kicks and crushing injuries from large animals and **zoonotic infections**. Prevention includes reading animal body language, using **low-stress handling**, proper restraint, **muzzles** when appropriate (never on an animal with breathing difficulty or vomiting), team lifts for heavy animals, safe sharps disposal and hand hygiene after every patient.',
'Other hazards include **radiation** from X-rays (lead aprons, thyroid shields, dosimeters, never holding animals in the beam when it can be avoided), **anesthetic waste gases** (scavenging systems), **chemicals** (formalin, disinfectants—follow Safety Data Sheets), **compressed gas cylinders** and **sharps**. With large animals, stay out of the **kick zone**, approach horses at the shoulder and never stand directly behind a horse or cow.',
sort('Hazard prevention',['Safe practice','Unsafe practice'],[['Using a muzzle on a frightened dog with normal breathing',0],['Muzzling a dog that is vomiting',1],['Wearing a lead apron and thyroid shield for X-rays',0],['Standing directly behind a horse',1],['Lifting a 70-lb dog with a teammate',0],['Recapping a used needle with two hands',1]])]],
['Careers and pathways',[
'Veterinary careers include small-animal, mixed and large-animal practice, equine and exotic medicine, **shelter medicine**, **public health** (food safety, zoonotic disease surveillance), the military, research and industry. The **AAVMC**, which sponsors this event, represents veterinary schools. Students prepare with strong science courses, animal and veterinary experience, and communication skills.',
kase('Ethical judgment',{a:{text:'A client calls asking for antibiotics for a dog the clinic has never seen. What is the right response? (Scenario invented for practice.)',choices:[['Explain that a veterinarian must examine the dog first to establish a VCPR','b','Correct. Prescribing requires a valid VCPR.',true],['Dispense the antibiotics to be helpful','b','That bypasses legal and ethical requirements.',false]]},b:{text:'During the exam, the dog bites a technician’s hand, breaking the skin. What should happen?',choices:[['Wash the wound, report it, seek medical care and follow rabies-exposure protocols','c','Correct. Bites must be treated and reported.',true],['Ignore it because the dog looks healthy','c','Bites can cause serious infection and must be reported.',false]]},c:{text:'The clinic documents the bite, verifies the dog’s rabies vaccination and reviews its handling plan.',end:true}})]],
["Communicating with clients",["Clients are partners in their animals’ care. Greet them warmly, use the pet’s name, explain findings and costs in plain language and check understanding with **teach-back**. Provide written **estimates** and discharge instructions, and obtain consent before procedures. Many owners see their pets as family, so **compassion** matters most during serious illness and **euthanasia** discussions; offer privacy, time and grief resources.","Communication within the team—using clear handoffs, repeating back medication orders and documenting everything—prevents errors. Veterinary professionals also educate the public about responsible pet ownership, vaccination, parasite control, spaying and neutering and safe interactions with animals, especially for children.",{"t":"check","q":"A client is confused about how to give eye drops at home. What is the best approach?","options":["Demonstrate, then ask the client to show you (teach-back)","Hand them the bottle and leave","Tell them to search online","Skip the medication"],"why":"Teach-back confirms understanding."}]]
],takeaways:['Round One: 50 questions across large and small animals (A&P 24%); Round Two: instruments, breeds, operative site prep, fecal flotation, dog lifting and restraint, surgical pack and feline temperature prep, using stuffed animals.','Veterinarians (DVM) diagnose, prescribe and operate; credentialed technicians (VTNE) monitor anesthesia, take radiographs and run labs; assistants help with restraint and care.','A VCPR is required before treatment; follow the Five Freedoms, drug and withdrawal laws; prevent bites, back injuries, needlesticks, radiation exposure and zoonoses.'],terms:[['VCPR','The veterinarian–client–patient relationship required before treatment.'],['VTNE','The national exam for veterinary technicians.'],['Five Freedoms','Basic standards for animal welfare.']],mcqs:[q('What percentage of the test is anatomy and physiology?','24%','6%','10%','14%','The largest area.'),q('Which degree do veterinarians earn?','DVM','MD','DPT','PharmD','Doctor of Veterinary Medicine.'),q('Which exam do veterinary technicians pass?','VTNE','NCLEX','PANCE','NPTE','Veterinary Technician National Examination.'),q('What must exist before a veterinarian prescribes?','A valid veterinarian–client–patient relationship','A phone call','A written request from a neighbor','Nothing','VCPR.'),q('Which is one of the Five Freedoms?','Freedom from fear and distress','Freedom from vaccines','Freedom from exercise','Freedom from grooming','Animal welfare.'),q('When should a dog NOT be muzzled?','When it is vomiting or has breathing difficulty','When it is frightened','When it is getting a nail trim','When it has bitten before','Airway safety.'),q('What are live animals replaced with in Round Two?','Stuffed animals','Robots','Cadavers','Photos only','No live animals.'),q('What is a withdrawal time?','The wait after drug use before milk or meat may be used','The time to remove stitches','Recovery time after surgery','A vacation period','Food safety.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe the roles of the veterinarian, credentialed veterinary technician and veterinary assistant, and explain three ethical or legal responsibilities and three safety practices in a veterinary clinic.',rubric:[['Roles',['veterinarian','diagnose','surgery','technician','anesthesia','radiographs','assistant','restraint']],['Ethics and law',['vcpr','informed consent','confidential','controlled','withdrawal','reportable','five freedoms']],['Safety',['bite','muzzle','lifting','needle','radiation','lead','zoonotic','hand hygiene']],['Application',['examine','report','document','welfare']]],model:'The veterinarian holds a DVM, diagnoses illness, prescribes medication, performs surgery and makes treatment decisions. The credentialed veterinary technician has completed an accredited program and passed the VTNE; technicians monitor anesthesia, take radiographs, run laboratory tests, place catheters, give medications as directed and educate clients. Veterinary assistants help restrain animals, feed and care for patients and keep kennels and equipment clean. Ethically and legally, a veterinarian must have a valid veterinarian–client–patient relationship—having examined the animal and taken responsibility for its care—before diagnosing or prescribing; the owner must give informed consent before surgery or anesthesia; and controlled drugs must be logged and secured, with withdrawal times followed for food animals and reportable diseases such as rabies reported. The team protects animal welfare using the Five Freedoms and keeps client information confidential. For safety, staff use low-stress handling and muzzles when appropriate (never on an animal that is vomiting or struggling to breathe) to prevent bites; lift heavy dogs with a teammate using good body mechanics; dispose of needles in sharps containers without recapping; wear lead aprons and thyroid shields during radiography; and wash hands after every patient to prevent zoonotic infections.'},sources:[RULES,AAVMC,BLS_VET,BLS_VT,AVMA_ETHICS]};

export const V2={title:'Anatomy and physiology I: body organization, musculoskeletal, cardiovascular and respiratory systems',hook:'A cat’s heart beats about twice as fast as a large dog’s, and a horse breathes only about 8 to 16 times a minute. Knowing what is normal for each species—and why—lets veterinary staff spot illness early. Anatomy and physiology is the largest part of the test (24%), so it gets two lessons.',objectives:['Use directional and anatomical terms for animals.','Describe the skeletal and muscular systems, including species differences.','Explain the cardiovascular and respiratory systems and blood.','State normal vital signs for common species.'],sections:[
['Directional terms and body organization',[
'Animal anatomy uses its own directional terms because most animals stand on four legs. **Cranial** means toward the head and **caudal** toward the tail; **rostral** means toward the nose (on the head); **dorsal** is toward the back and **ventral** toward the belly; **medial** is toward the midline and **lateral** away from it; **proximal** is closer to the body and **distal** farther away. On the limbs, **palmar** describes the back of the front foot and **plantar** the back of the hind foot.',
'Cells form **tissues** (epithelial, connective, muscle and nervous), tissues form **organs** and organs form **systems**. The body cavities are the **thoracic cavity** (heart and lungs), separated by the **diaphragm** from the **abdominal cavity** (digestive organs, kidneys, liver, spleen), with the **pelvic cavity** behind it.',
match('Directional terms',[['Cranial','Toward the head'],['Caudal','Toward the tail'],['Dorsal','Toward the back'],['Ventral','Toward the belly'],['Rostral','Toward the nose'],['Distal','Farther from the body'],['Palmar','Back of the front foot'],['Plantar','Back of the hind foot']])]],
['The skeletal and muscular systems',[
'The skeleton supports the body, protects organs, stores minerals and makes blood cells in red marrow. The **axial skeleton** includes the skull, vertebrae, ribs and sternum; the **appendicular skeleton** includes the limbs and girdles. Vertebrae are grouped into **cervical** (7 in almost all mammals), **thoracic**, **lumbar**, **sacral** and **coccygeal (tail)** regions; the numbers of thoracic and lumbar vertebrae vary by species. Dogs and cats walk on their toes (**digitigrade**), horses and cattle on the tips of their toes (**unguligrade**, hooves) and humans and bears on the soles (**plantigrade**).',
'**Skeletal muscle** moves bones and is voluntary; **cardiac muscle** is found only in the heart; **smooth muscle** lines organs and blood vessels and is involuntary. **Tendons** attach muscle to bone and **ligaments** attach bone to bone. Common landmarks include the **stifle** (the knee of the hind leg), the **hock** (the hind ankle), the **carpus** (front “wrist”) and the **withers** (top of the shoulders, used to measure height in horses and dogs).',
sort('Axial or appendicular?',['Axial skeleton','Appendicular skeleton'],[['Skull',0],['Femur',1],['Ribs',0],['Scapula',1],['Vertebrae',0],['Radius and ulna',1]]),
match('Animal landmarks',[['Stifle','Knee of the hind limb'],['Hock','Ankle of the hind limb'],['Carpus','Wrist of the front limb'],['Withers','Top of the shoulders'],['Digitigrade','Walks on the toes, like dogs and cats'],['Unguligrade','Walks on hooves, like horses']])]],
['The cardiovascular system and blood',[
'Mammals and birds have a **four-chambered heart**: the right side pumps blood to the lungs (**pulmonary circulation**) and the left side pumps it to the body (**systemic circulation**). **Arteries** carry blood away from the heart and **veins** return it. The heart rate is felt as a **pulse**—in dogs and cats, usually at the **femoral artery** on the inside of the thigh. Common veins for blood draws are the **cephalic** (front leg), **saphenous** (hind leg) and **jugular** (neck) veins.',
'Blood contains **red blood cells** (oxygen transport), **white blood cells** (immunity) and **platelets** (clotting) in **plasma**. A **packed cell volume (PCV)** measures the percentage of red cells. **Mucous membrane color** (pink is normal) and **capillary refill time** (normally under about **2 seconds**) help assess circulation: pale gums suggest anemia or shock, blue gums low oxygen and bright red gums heatstroke or toxicity.',
match('Circulation clues',[['Pink gums, CRT under 2 seconds','Normal circulation'],['Pale gums','Anemia or shock'],['Blue (cyanotic) gums','Low oxygen'],['Bright red gums','Heatstroke or some toxins'],['Femoral artery','Common pulse site in dogs and cats'],['Cephalic vein','Common blood draw site on the front leg']])]],
['The respiratory system',[
'Air enters through the **nostrils (nares)**, passes the **nasal cavity**, **pharynx**, **larynx** and **trachea**, then branches into **bronchi**, **bronchioles** and **alveoli**, where oxygen enters the blood and carbon dioxide leaves. The **diaphragm** is the main breathing muscle. Horses are **obligate nasal breathers**—they cannot breathe through the mouth—so nasal obstruction is serious. **Brachycephalic** (short-nosed) breeds such as Bulldogs, Pugs and Persians often have narrow airways and overheat easily.',
"Birds have a very different respiratory system: **air sacs** move air through rigid lungs in one direction, so birds breathe efficiently but are very sensitive to fumes such as overheated nonstick cookware and aerosol sprays. Holding a bird too tightly around the body can stop it from breathing, because birds have no diaphragm.",
'Respiratory rate is counted by watching the chest rise and fall for a full minute (or 15 seconds × 4) while the animal is calm; panting dogs cannot be counted accurately. Signs of respiratory distress include open-mouth breathing in cats, extended neck, flared nostrils and abdominal effort.',
chk('Why are horses especially at risk from nasal obstruction?',['They are obligate nasal breathers','They have no trachea','They breathe only through the mouth','They have four lungs'],'Horses cannot breathe through their mouths.')]],
['Normal vital signs',[
'Approximate adult teaching ranges (references vary slightly): **dog**—temperature **100.5–102.5 °F**, heart rate about **60–140** beats per minute (small dogs faster), respiratory rate about **10–30**; **cat**—temperature **100.5–102.5 °F**, heart rate about **140–220**, respiratory rate about **20–30**; **horse**—temperature about **99–101 °F**, heart rate about **28–44**, respiratory rate about **8–16**; **cattle**—temperature about **101–102.5 °F**, heart rate about **40–80**, respiratory rate about **10–30**. Small animals have faster rates: a **hamster’s** normal respiratory rate is about **74** breaths per minute in Lawhead and Baker.',
'Temperatures in animals are taken **rectally** with a lubricated digital thermometer. A fever may signal infection or inflammation; a low temperature (hypothermia) is common after anesthesia and in shock. Always compare readings with the species’ normal range and the animal’s history.',
"Other measurements matter too. **Weight** and **body condition score** are recorded at every visit because small changes can signal disease, and drug doses are calculated per kilogram (1 kg = 2.2 lb). **Hydration** is assessed with skin tent, moist gums and eye position; dehydrated animals have tacky gums and slow skin return.",
{t:'table',caption:'Approximate adult vital signs (teaching ranges)',head:['Species','Temperature (°F)','Heart rate (bpm)','Respiratory rate (per min)'],rows:[['Dog','100.5–102.5','60–140','10–30'],['Cat','100.5–102.5','140–220','20–30'],['Horse','99–101','28–44','8–16'],['Cattle','101–102.5','40–80','10–30']]},
sort('Normal or abnormal for a cat?',['Within normal range','Outside normal range'],[['Temperature 101.5 °F',0],['Heart rate 180',0],['Temperature 104.5 °F',1],['Heart rate 90',1],['Respiratory rate 24',0],['Open-mouth breathing',1]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Reading vital signs',{a:{text:'A large-breed dog has a temperature of 103.8 °F, heart rate 130 and pale gums with a capillary refill time of 3 seconds. (Scenario invented for practice.) Which findings are abnormal?',choices:[['Fever, pale gums and prolonged capillary refill','b','Correct. These suggest illness and possible poor perfusion.',true],['Only the heart rate','b','The heart rate is within range for many dogs; the other findings are not.',false]]},b:{text:'What should the technician do next?',choices:[['Record the findings and alert the veterinarian promptly','c','Correct. Abnormal circulation needs quick evaluation.',true],['Wait until tomorrow','c','Pale gums and slow refill can signal shock.',false]]},c:{text:'The veterinarian examines the dog and begins diagnostics. Accurate vital signs started the process.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Directional terms: cranial, caudal, rostral, dorsal, ventral, proximal, distal, palmar and plantar; body cavities are thoracic, abdominal and pelvic.','Axial and appendicular skeletons; digitigrade, unguligrade and plantigrade stances; stifle, hock, carpus and withers; skeletal, cardiac and smooth muscle.','Four-chambered heart; pink gums and CRT under 2 seconds are normal; know approximate vital signs for dogs, cats, horses and cattle; horses are obligate nasal breathers.'],terms:[['Capillary refill time','Time for gum color to return after pressing, normally under about 2 seconds.'],['Stifle','The knee joint of an animal’s hind limb.'],['Brachycephalic','Having a short, flat face, as in Bulldogs and Persians.']],mcqs:[q('What is the normal respiratory rate for a hamster (Lawhead and Baker)?','74','12','26','90','HOSA sample question.'),q('Toward the tail is described as:','Caudal','Cranial','Rostral','Dorsal','Cranial is toward the head.'),q('A normal capillary refill time is under about:','2 seconds','10 seconds','30 seconds','1 minute','Pink gums too.'),q('The stifle is the:','Knee of the hind limb','Ankle of the hind limb','Front wrist','Top of the shoulder','Hock is the ankle.'),q('Normal adult dog temperature is about:','100.5–102.5 °F','96–98 °F','104–106 °F','98.6 °F exactly','Rectal.'),q('Which species is an obligate nasal breather?','Horse','Dog','Cat','Pig','Cannot mouth-breathe.'),q('Pale gums may indicate:','Anemia or shock','Normal health','Heatstroke','A full stomach','Poor perfusion.'),q('Dogs and cats walk on their toes and are called:','Digitigrade','Plantigrade','Unguligrade','Bipedal','Toe walkers.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how a veterinary technician assesses circulation and respiration in a dog, including normal values, and describe how these findings might differ in a cat and a horse.',rubric:[['Circulation',['pulse','femoral','heart rate','mucous membrane','pink','capillary refill','2 seconds']],['Respiration',['chest','rise','full minute','respiratory rate','distress','panting']],['Normal values',['100.5','102.5','60','140','10','30']],['Species differences',['cat','140','220','horse','28','44','8','16','nasal']]],model:'To assess circulation, I count the heart rate with a stethoscope or feel the pulse at the femoral artery on the inside of the thigh, check that the pulse is strong and regular, and look at the gums: normal mucous membranes are pink and moist, and when pressed they should refill in under about 2 seconds. Pale gums or a slow capillary refill suggest anemia or shock, blue gums suggest low oxygen and bright red gums can indicate heatstroke. For respiration, I watch the chest rise and fall while the dog is calm for a full minute, noting the rate, depth and effort; panting makes counting inaccurate. Normal adult values for a dog are roughly a temperature of 100.5–102.5 °F, a heart rate of 60–140 beats per minute (faster in small dogs) and a respiratory rate of 10–30 breaths per minute. A cat has the same temperature range but a faster heart rate, about 140–220, and a respiratory rate of about 20–30; open-mouth breathing in a cat signals distress. A horse has a lower temperature range of about 99–101 °F, a slow heart rate of about 28–44 and a respiratory rate of about 8–16, and because horses are obligate nasal breathers, any nasal obstruction is serious.'},sources:[RULES,MERCK,AAVMC]};

export const V3={title:'Anatomy and physiology II: digestive, urinary, reproductive, nervous and integumentary systems',hook:'A cow has one stomach with four compartments; a horse ferments its food in an enormous cecum; a cat cannot make taurine and will go blind without it in its diet. Digestive design shapes how each species is fed, medicated and treated for colic or bloat. This lesson finishes anatomy and physiology with digestion, reproduction and the remaining systems.',objectives:['Compare monogastric, ruminant and hindgut-fermenting digestive systems.','Describe urinary anatomy and common urinary problems.','State reproductive terms and approximate gestation lengths for common species.','Describe the nervous, integumentary and endocrine systems and dental formulas.'],sections:[
['Digestive systems compared',[
'**Monogastric** animals such as dogs, cats, pigs and humans have a **simple stomach**. **Carnivores** (cats are **obligate carnivores**) have short digestive tracts; **omnivores** such as dogs and pigs eat both plants and animals. **Ruminants**—cattle, sheep and goats—have one stomach with **four compartments**: the **rumen** (large fermentation vat where microbes break down cellulose), **reticulum** (“honeycomb”; traps heavy objects such as wire—hardware disease), **omasum** (absorbs water) and **abomasum** (the “true stomach” with acid and enzymes). Ruminants **chew cud** (regurgitate and rechew food).',
'**Hindgut fermenters**—horses and rabbits—have a simple stomach but ferment fiber in a large **cecum** and colon. Horses **cannot vomit**, so gas or blockage can cause life-threatening **colic**. Rabbits eat special soft feces (**cecotropes**) to reabsorb nutrients. Birds have a **crop** (storage) and **gizzard** (grinding). Large deep-chested dogs can develop **gastric dilatation-volvulus (GDV, bloat)**, an emergency in which the stomach twists.',
match('Digestive structures',[['Rumen','Large fermentation compartment in ruminants'],['Reticulum','Honeycomb compartment; traps hardware'],['Omasum','Absorbs water'],['Abomasum','True stomach of ruminants'],['Cecum','Fermentation chamber in horses and rabbits'],['Gizzard','Grinding organ in birds'],['Crop','Food storage pouch in birds'],['GDV','Twisted stomach in deep-chested dogs']])]],
['Teeth and dental formulas',[
'A **dental formula** lists the number of incisors (I), canines (C), premolars (P) and molars (M) in one half of the upper and lower jaw, then doubles it. Adult **dogs** have **42** permanent teeth and adult **cats** have **30**. Puppies and kittens have deciduous (baby) teeth that are replaced by about 6 months of age. Horses have **hypsodont** teeth that keep erupting and must be **floated** (filed) to remove sharp points; rabbits and rodents have continuously growing incisors.',
'**Periodontal disease** is the most common health problem in adult dogs and cats; it causes bad breath, gum inflammation and tooth loss and can affect organs. Prevention includes tooth brushing, dental diets and regular dental cleanings under anesthesia.',
chk('How many permanent teeth does an adult dog have?',['42','30','32','28'],'Adult cats have 30.')]],
['The urinary system',[
'The **kidneys** filter blood and make urine; urine flows through the **ureters** to the **bladder** and out the **urethra**. Kidneys also regulate fluids, electrolytes and blood pressure. **Chronic kidney disease** is common in older cats and causes increased thirst and urination and weight loss. **Urinary stones (uroliths)** and **urethral obstruction**—most common in **male cats**—are emergencies: a male cat straining in the litter box without producing urine needs immediate care because potassium can rise to life-threatening levels.',
'**Urinalysis** measures urine specific gravity (concentration), pH, protein, glucose, blood and crystals and examines sediment under a microscope. Cats should be encouraged to drink water; wet food and fountains help.',
"Dogs commonly develop **bladder stones** and **urinary tract infections**; signs include frequent urination, straining, blood in the urine and accidents in the house. Urine samples should be tested within about 30–60 minutes or refrigerated, because crystals and bacteria can change over time.",
sort('Emergency or routine?',['Emergency','Routine follow-up'],[['Male cat straining with no urine',0],['Older cat drinking a little more water this month',1],['Dog with bloody urine and severe pain',0],['Annual urinalysis in a healthy dog',1],['Cat that has not urinated in 24 hours',0]])]],
['The reproductive system',[
'Terms differ by species: a female dog is a **bitch** and a male a **dog**; a female cat a **queen** and male a **tom**; a female horse a **mare** and male a **stallion** (castrated male: **gelding**); a female cattle animal a **cow**, a young female a **heifer**, a male a **bull** and a castrated male a **steer**; a female pig a **sow**, a young female a **gilt**, a male a **boar** and a castrated male a **barrow**. Giving birth is called **whelping** (dogs), **queening** (cats), **foaling** (horses), **calving** (cattle) and **farrowing** (pigs).',
'Approximate **gestation lengths**: **cat about 58–65 days**, **dog about 63 days**, **pig about 114 days** (“3 months, 3 weeks, 3 days”), **sheep and goats about 150 days**, **cattle about 283 days** and **horses about 340 days**. **Spaying** (ovariohysterectomy) and **neutering** (castration) prevent unwanted litters and some diseases and behaviors.',
{t:'table',caption:'Approximate gestation lengths',head:['Species','Gestation','Birth term'],rows:[['Cat','58–65 days','Queening'],['Dog','About 63 days','Whelping'],['Pig','About 114 days','Farrowing'],['Sheep and goat','About 150 days','Lambing / kidding'],['Cattle','About 283 days','Calving'],['Horse','About 340 days','Foaling']]},
match('Species terms',[['Queen','Female cat'],['Mare','Female horse'],['Gelding','Castrated male horse'],['Heifer','Young female cattle'],['Sow','Female pig'],['Steer','Castrated male cattle'],['Whelping','Dog giving birth'],['Farrowing','Pig giving birth']])]],
['Nervous, integumentary and endocrine systems',[
'The **nervous system** includes the brain, spinal cord and nerves. **Intervertebral disc disease** is common in long-backed breeds such as Dachshunds and can cause back pain or paralysis. Seizures may result from epilepsy, toxins or low blood sugar. The **integumentary system**—skin, hair, claws, hooves, horns and glands—protects the body; skin problems such as **allergies**, **fleas** and **ringworm** are very common.',
'The **endocrine system** uses hormones: **diabetes mellitus** occurs in dogs and cats; **hyperthyroidism** is common in older cats (weight loss with a big appetite); **hypothyroidism** occurs in dogs (weight gain, hair loss); **Cushing’s disease** (excess cortisol) causes pot belly, thirst and hair loss in dogs. **Addison’s disease** (low cortisol) can cause collapse.',
"The **eyes and ears** are also part of the exam. Common eye problems include conjunctivitis, corneal ulcers, cataracts and **glaucoma** (painful, red eye with a cloudy cornea—an emergency). Dogs with long, floppy ears are prone to **otitis externa** (ear infections) from yeast and bacteria, and **ear mites** are common in kittens.",
sort('Which system or disease?',['Nervous','Integumentary','Endocrine'],[['Dachshund with sudden hind-leg weakness',0],['Itchy skin with fleas',1],['Older cat losing weight while eating more',2],['Seizures',0],['Ringworm lesions',1],['Dog with pot belly, thirst and hair loss',2]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Digestive emergency',{a:{text:'A Great Dane has a swollen abdomen, unproductive retching and restlessness after a large meal. (Scenario invented for practice.) What do you suspect?',choices:[['Gastric dilatation-volvulus (bloat)','b','Correct. Deep-chested breeds are at risk.',true],['Normal fullness','b','Unproductive retching with a swollen belly is an emergency.',false]]},b:{text:'What should happen next?',choices:[['Alert the veterinarian immediately for emergency treatment','c','Correct. GDV is fatal without rapid treatment.',true],['Give food to settle the stomach','c','Food worsens the problem.',false]]},c:{text:'The veterinarian decompresses the stomach and performs surgery, including a gastropexy to prevent recurrence.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Monogastric (dog, cat, pig), ruminant (rumen, reticulum, omasum, abomasum) and hindgut fermenters (horse, rabbit cecum); horses cannot vomit; GDV is a canine emergency.','Dogs have 42 adult teeth and cats 30; horses’ teeth are floated; periodontal disease is very common; male cats with urethral obstruction are emergencies.','Gestation: cat 58–65 days, dog ~63, pig ~114, sheep/goat ~150, cattle ~283, horse ~340; know species terms; IVDD, allergies, hyperthyroid cats, hypothyroid and Cushing’s dogs.'],terms:[['Ruminant','An animal with a four-compartment stomach that chews cud.'],['Gestation','The length of pregnancy.'],['Gastric dilatation-volvulus','An emergency in which a dog’s stomach fills with gas and twists.']],mcqs:[q('How many days is the average length of pregnancy for a cat?','58–65','15–20','30–35','90–112','HOSA sample question.'),q('Which compartment is the ruminant’s true stomach?','Abomasum','Rumen','Reticulum','Omasum','Acid and enzymes.'),q('Horses ferment fiber mainly in the:','Cecum','Rumen','Crop','Gizzard','Hindgut fermenters.'),q('How many permanent teeth does an adult cat have?','30','42','28','36','Dogs have 42.'),q('Which animal is most at risk for urethral obstruction?','Male cat','Female dog','Mare','Heifer','Emergency.'),q('A castrated male horse is a:','Gelding','Stallion','Mare','Barrow','Steer is cattle.'),q('Approximate gestation for cattle is:','283 days','63 days','114 days','340 days','Horse is about 340.'),q('An older cat with weight loss and a big appetite may have:','Hyperthyroidism','Hypothyroidism','Addison’s disease','GDV','Common in senior cats.')],frq:{prompt:'Compare the digestive systems of a dog, a cow and a horse, and explain how these differences affect feeding and the risk of digestive emergencies.',rubric:[['Dog',['monogastric','simple stomach','omnivore','gdv','bloat','deep-chested']],['Cow',['ruminant','rumen','reticulum','omasum','abomasum','cud','hardware']],['Horse',['hindgut','cecum','cannot vomit','colic','fiber']],['Feeding implications',['small meals','forage','gradual','fiber','water']]],model:'A dog is a monogastric omnivore with a simple stomach and a relatively short digestive tract, so it digests a mixed diet of animal and plant ingredients in the stomach and small intestine. Large, deep-chested dogs are at risk of gastric dilatation-volvulus, in which the stomach fills with gas and twists; feeding smaller meals, avoiding vigorous exercise right after eating and preventive gastropexy reduce the risk. A cow is a ruminant with one stomach divided into four compartments: the rumen, where microbes ferment cellulose; the reticulum, which traps heavy objects and can be punctured by swallowed wire (hardware disease); the omasum, which absorbs water; and the abomasum, the true stomach. Cows chew cud and need large amounts of forage, and diet changes must be gradual to avoid problems such as bloat or acidosis. A horse is a hindgut fermenter with a small simple stomach and a very large cecum and colon where fiber is fermented. Horses cannot vomit, so gas, sudden diet changes or impactions can cause colic, a potentially fatal emergency; they need frequent access to forage, plenty of water and gradual feed changes.'},sources:[RULES,OSB_DIGEST,MERCK]};

export const V4={title:'Basic nutrients and animal behavior',hook:'Chocolate, grapes, xylitol and lilies can all kill pets, and a cat fed only dog food can go blind. Good nutrition prevents disease, and understanding behavior prevents bites and stress. Basic nutrients (10%) and animal behavior (6%) are tested together here.',objectives:['Describe the six classes of nutrients and their functions.','Explain species-specific needs, such as cats’ need for taurine.','Identify common toxic foods and plants.','Read animal body language and apply low-stress handling.'],sections:[
['The six nutrients',[
'Animals need **water**, **carbohydrates**, **fats (lipids)**, **proteins**, **vitamins** and **minerals**. **Water** is the most important nutrient—animals can survive longer without food than without water. **Carbohydrates** provide energy and fiber; **fats** supply concentrated energy (more than twice the calories per gram of carbohydrate or protein), essential fatty acids and fat-soluble vitamins; **proteins** are made of **amino acids** and build muscle, enzymes and antibodies.',
'**Vitamins** are **fat-soluble** (A, D, E, K—stored in the body and toxic in excess) or **water-soluble** (B vitamins and C—not stored). **Minerals** such as calcium and phosphorus build bones (their ratio matters, especially in growing animals and reptiles), and electrolytes such as sodium and potassium control fluid balance. Most commercial pet foods labeled **“complete and balanced”** meet AAFCO standards for a life stage.',
sort('Nutrient function',['Water','Protein','Fat','Vitamin or mineral'],[['Most important nutrient',0],['Builds muscle and antibodies',1],['Most concentrated energy source',2],['Calcium for bone',3],['Made of amino acids',1],['Carries fat-soluble vitamins',2]])]],
['Species-specific needs',[
'**Cats are obligate carnivores**: they need **taurine** (deficiency causes blindness and heart disease), **arachidonic acid**, preformed **vitamin A** (they cannot convert beta-carotene) and more protein than dogs. Dog food does not meet these needs. **Ruminants** need fiber for rumen health; **horses** need constant forage; **rabbits and guinea pigs** need hay, and **guinea pigs** must receive **vitamin C** in the diet because they cannot make it.',
'Life stage matters: growing puppies and kittens need more calories and protein; pregnant and nursing animals need more energy; older animals may need fewer calories. **Obesity** is the most common nutritional problem in pets and contributes to arthritis, diabetes and shortened lives. **Body condition scoring** (often on a 1–9 scale, with 4–5 ideal) uses rib feel, waist and abdominal tuck.',
match('Special needs',[['Taurine','Required by cats; prevents blindness and heart disease'],['Vitamin C in the diet','Required by guinea pigs'],['Constant forage','Needed by horses'],['Body condition score 4–5 of 9','Ideal weight'],['Obesity','Most common pet nutritional problem'],['Complete and balanced','Meets AAFCO nutrient profile']])]],
['Toxic foods and plants',[
'Common toxins for dogs include **chocolate** (theobromine; darker chocolate is more toxic), **xylitol** (artificial sweetener; causes low blood sugar and liver failure), **grapes and raisins** (kidney failure), **onions and garlic** (red blood cell damage), **macadamia nuts**, alcohol and caffeine. For cats, **lilies** (even pollen or vase water) cause **kidney failure**, and **acetaminophen** is deadly. Many human medications, rodenticides and antifreeze (**ethylene glycol**) poison pets.',
'If a poisoning is suspected, call the veterinarian or an animal poison control center right away, bring the product label and do **not** induce vomiting unless instructed. Keep toxins out of reach and educate clients.',
sort('Toxic or generally safe?',['Toxic','Generally safe in small amounts'],[['Xylitol gum for a dog',0],['Plain cooked chicken for a dog',1],['Lily pollen for a cat',0],['Grapes for a dog',0],['Plain canned pumpkin for a dog',1],['Acetaminophen for a cat',0]])]],
['Reading body language',[
'Recognizing fear and stress prevents bites. In **dogs**, warning signs include lip licking, yawning, “whale eye” (whites of the eyes showing), a tucked tail, ears pinned back, a stiff body, freezing, raised hackles, growling and lifting a lip. A wagging tail does not always mean friendliness. In **cats**, signs of fear or aggression include flattened ears, dilated pupils, a crouched posture, a lashing or puffed tail, hissing and growling.',
'Livestock respond to the handler’s position: the **flight zone** is the animal’s personal space, and the **point of balance** (usually at the shoulder) determines which way it moves—stepping behind the point of balance moves an animal forward. Horses are prey animals that spook easily; approach calmly from the side at the shoulder, speaking softly.',
sort('Relaxed or stressed dog?',['Relaxed','Stressed or fearful'],[['Soft, loose body and relaxed mouth',0],['Whale eye and tucked tail',1],['Lip licking and yawning during an exam',1],['Play bow',0],['Freezing with a stiff body',1],['Raised hackles and a growl',1]])]],
['Low-stress handling and behavior problems',[
'**Fear Free** and **low-stress handling** principles reduce fear, anxiety and stress: use treats, calm voices and slow movements; minimize restraint; use towels for cats; keep cats away from barking dogs; and use **pheromone** products. For cats, the American Association of Feline Practitioners encourages “go slow to go fast”—calmer cats are easier and safer to examine.',
'Common behavior problems include separation anxiety, aggression, house soiling, inappropriate scratching and noise phobias. **Positive reinforcement** (rewarding desired behavior) is the recommended training method. Early **socialization** of puppies and kittens (roughly 3–14 weeks for puppies and 2–7 weeks for kittens) prevents many problems.',
"Normal species behavior also matters for welfare: cats need places to **scratch, climb and hide**; dogs need exercise, play and mental stimulation; horses and cattle are herd animals that become stressed when isolated; rabbits are prey animals that can be injured if they kick while held incorrectly—always support the hindquarters. **Environmental enrichment** reduces boredom and stress-related behaviors.",
kase('Handling a fearful dog',{a:{text:'A dog shows whale eye, a tucked tail and lip licking on the exam table. (Scenario invented for practice.) What is the best approach?',choices:[['Slow down, offer treats, use minimal restraint and consider a muzzle if needed','b','Correct. Low-stress handling prevents bites.',true],['Hold the dog down firmly and hurry','b','Force increases fear and bite risk.',false]]},b:{text:'The dog growls when its paw is touched. What does the growl mean?',choices:[['A warning—stop, reassess and change the approach','c','Correct. Never punish a growl; it is communication.',true],['The dog is happy','c','A growl signals discomfort.',false]]},c:{text:'The team uses treats and gentle handling, finishes the exam and notes the dog’s preferences for future visits.',end:true}})]],
["Feeding programs and livestock nutrition",["Livestock rations are balanced for **energy, protein, fiber, minerals and vitamins** according to species, age and production (growth, pregnancy, lactation or work). Cattle and sheep eat **forages** (pasture, hay, silage) plus **concentrates** (grains) when needed; sudden increases in grain can cause **rumen acidosis** and **bloat**. Horses eat mostly forage and should get grain in small, frequent meals; sudden diet changes can trigger **colic** or **laminitis**. Clean, fresh **water** must always be available.","Feed labels list **guaranteed analysis** (minimum protein and fat, maximum fiber and moisture), ingredients in descending order by weight and feeding directions. Treats should make up no more than about **10%** of a pet’s daily calories. Veterinarians and veterinary nutritionists create special diets for kidney disease, urinary stones, allergies, obesity and diabetes.",{"t":"w","kind":"sort","title":"Good or risky feeding practice?","buckets":["Good practice","Risky practice"],"items":[["Changing a horse’s feed gradually",0],["Giving a cow a sudden large grain meal",1],["Constant access to clean water",0],["Treats making up half of a dog’s calories",1],["Feeding hay to rabbits daily",0],["Feeding a cat dog food for months",1]]}]]
],takeaways:['Six nutrients: water (most important), carbohydrates, fats (most energy), proteins (amino acids), vitamins (fat-soluble A, D, E, K vs water-soluble B, C) and minerals.','Cats need taurine, arachidonic acid and preformed vitamin A; guinea pigs need dietary vitamin C; horses need forage; obesity is the top pet nutrition problem; xylitol, chocolate, grapes, onions and lilies are toxic.','Read stress signals (whale eye, lip licking, flattened ears) and use low-stress handling, positive reinforcement and the flight zone and point of balance for livestock.'],terms:[['Obligate carnivore','An animal, such as a cat, that must eat animal tissue to meet its needs.'],['Taurine','An amino acid cats require in their diet.'],['Flight zone','An animal’s personal space; entering it makes the animal move away.']],mcqs:[q('Which nutrient is the most important?','Water','Protein','Fat','Vitamin C','Survival depends on it.'),q('Cats must get which amino acid in their diet?','Taurine','Glycine','Alanine','Glutamine','Deficiency causes blindness and heart disease.'),q('Which animal must get vitamin C in its diet?','Guinea pig','Dog','Cat','Horse','Cannot make it.'),q('Which sweetener is toxic to dogs?','Xylitol','Sucrose','Honey','Corn syrup','Causes low blood sugar.'),q('Which plant is especially toxic to cats?','Lily','Catnip','Wheatgrass','Spider plant','Kidney failure.'),q('“Whale eye” in a dog signals:','Stress or fear','Happiness','Hunger','Sleepiness','Whites of the eyes showing.'),q('Which vitamins are fat-soluble?','A, D, E and K','B and C','Only C','None','Stored in the body.'),q('A dog with bumps on its face and muzzle that is licking its flank is most likely experiencing:','An allergic reaction','Bleeding','A torsion','Frostbite','HOSA sample question (Red Cross Pet First Aid).')],frq:{prompt:'A family has a new kitten, a guinea pig and an older overweight dog. Describe the key nutritional needs of each, three foods or plants they must keep away from their pets and how they should recognize stress in the dog and kitten.',rubric:[['Kitten',['obligate carnivore','taurine','arachidonic','vitamin a','kitten food','protein']],['Guinea pig and dog',['vitamin c','hay','obesity','body condition','calories','exercise']],['Toxins',['chocolate','xylitol','grapes','raisins','onions','lilies','acetaminophen']],['Stress signals',['whale eye','lip licking','tucked tail','flattened ears','hissing','dilated pupils','low-stress']]],model:'The kitten is an obligate carnivore, so it needs a complete and balanced kitten food with high protein and the nutrients cats cannot make, including taurine, arachidonic acid and preformed vitamin A; dog food would not meet these needs. The guinea pig needs unlimited hay for fiber and dental health plus a daily source of vitamin C, because guinea pigs cannot make it. The older dog is overweight, so the family should measure portions of an appropriate adult or senior diet, limit treats, increase gentle exercise and use body condition scoring—aiming for about 4–5 on a 9-point scale—to reduce strain on the joints and the risk of diabetes. They must keep chocolate, xylitol-containing gum and baked goods, grapes and raisins, onions and garlic away from the dog, lilies away from the cat, and never give acetaminophen to the kitten. Signs of stress in the dog include lip licking, yawning, whale eye, a tucked tail, a stiff body and growling; in the kitten, flattened ears, dilated pupils, a crouched posture, a puffed or lashing tail and hissing. When they see these signs, they should slow down, give space and use calm, treat-based, low-stress handling.'},sources:[RULES,WSAVA,OSB_NUTR,ASPCA_POISON,FEARFREE]};

export const V5={title:'Diseases, disease prevention and zoonoses',hook:'Rabies kills almost everyone who develops symptoms, yet it is almost completely preventable with vaccination. Parvovirus can wipe out a litter of puppies, and a single flea can carry tapeworm eggs. Preventing disease is the most important job in veterinary medicine—and it protects people too. Diseases and disease prevention (14%) and zoonoses (6%) make up 20% of the test.',objectives:['Describe how infectious diseases spread and how the immune system and vaccines prevent them.','Identify core vaccines and major infectious diseases of dogs, cats, horses and livestock.','Describe common internal and external parasites and their prevention.','Explain major zoonoses and how to prevent them.'],sections:[
['Disease basics and immunity',[
'Diseases may be **infectious** (caused by viruses, bacteria, fungi, parasites or prions) or **noninfectious** (genetic, nutritional, metabolic, toxic, cancer, trauma). Infectious agents spread by **direct contact**, **indirect contact** with **fomites** (contaminated objects such as bowls, leashes or clothing), **airborne** droplets, **vectors** (mosquitoes, ticks, fleas), **ingestion** and from mother to offspring. Breaking the chain with **sanitation**, **isolation** of sick animals and **biosecurity** prevents outbreaks.',
'**Active immunity** develops after infection or **vaccination**, when the animal makes its own antibodies and memory cells. **Passive immunity** is borrowed antibodies, such as those in **colostrum**—the first milk—which newborns must drink within hours of birth. Maternal antibodies fade over the first months, which is why puppies and kittens receive a **series of vaccines** every 3–4 weeks until about 16 weeks of age.',
match('Disease spread',[['Fomite','Contaminated object such as a bowl or leash'],['Vector','Living carrier such as a mosquito or tick'],['Colostrum','First milk rich in antibodies'],['Passive immunity','Borrowed antibodies'],['Active immunity','Animal makes its own antibodies'],['Biosecurity','Measures that keep disease out of a group of animals']])]],
['Core vaccines and diseases of dogs and cats',[
'**Core vaccines for dogs** protect against **rabies**, **canine distemper** (respiratory, GI and neurologic disease), **canine parvovirus** (severe vomiting and bloody diarrhea, especially in puppies) and **canine adenovirus** (infectious hepatitis), often combined as DAPP. Noncore vaccines (based on risk) include leptospirosis, Bordetella (kennel cough), Lyme disease and canine influenza. **Heartworm disease**, spread by mosquitoes, is prevented with monthly preventives.',
'**Core vaccines for cats** protect against **rabies** and **FVRCP**—feline viral rhinotracheitis (herpesvirus), calicivirus and **panleukopenia** (feline parvovirus). **Feline leukemia virus (FeLV)** vaccination is recommended for kittens and outdoor cats. **Feline immunodeficiency virus (FIV)**, spread mainly by bite wounds, has no widely used vaccine in the U.S.; testing and keeping cats indoors help prevent it.',
sort('Dog or cat disease?',['Dog','Cat'],[['Parvovirus causing bloody diarrhea in puppies',0],['Panleukopenia',1],['Distemper',0],['Feline leukemia virus',1],['Kennel cough (Bordetella)',0],['Calicivirus',1]])]],
['Large-animal diseases',[
'**Horses** receive core vaccines against **tetanus**, **rabies**, **Eastern and Western equine encephalomyelitis** and **West Nile virus** (the last two spread by mosquitoes). **Strangles** (*Streptococcus equi*) causes abscessed lymph nodes, and **equine infectious anemia** is detected by the **Coggins test**, often required for travel. **Colic** and **laminitis** (founder) are major noninfectious problems.',
'**Cattle** are vaccinated against clostridial diseases such as **blackleg**, and against respiratory viruses (IBR, BVD, PI3, BRSV) that contribute to **bovine respiratory disease** (shipping fever). **Mastitis** (udder infection) is the most costly disease of dairy cattle. **Foot-and-mouth disease** and **bovine spongiform encephalopathy (BSE)** are foreign or reportable diseases that would trigger government response. Biosecurity—quarantining new animals, cleaning trailers and limiting visitors—protects herds.',
match('Large-animal disease',[['Coggins test','Equine infectious anemia'],['Strangles','Abscessed lymph nodes in horses'],['Laminitis','Inflammation of the hoof laminae'],['Blackleg','Clostridial disease of cattle'],['Mastitis','Udder infection'],['West Nile virus','Mosquito-borne disease of horses and people']])]],
['Parasites',[
'**Internal parasites** include **roundworms** (common in puppies and kittens; zoonotic), **hookworms** (blood-sucking; can cause anemia and skin larva migrans in people), **whipworms**, **tapeworms** (often transmitted by swallowing **fleas**), **heartworms** (mosquitoes) and protozoa such as **Giardia** and **coccidia**. Diagnosis often uses a **fecal flotation** to find eggs, plus antigen tests for heartworms. Regular deworming and year-round preventives are recommended.',
'**External parasites** include **fleas** (itching, allergy dermatitis, anemia and tapeworms), **ticks** (Lyme disease, ehrlichiosis, anaplasmosis, Rocky Mountain spotted fever), **mites** (sarcoptic mange—highly contagious and zoonotic; demodectic mange; ear mites) and **lice**. Prevention products, environmental cleaning and regular checks control them.',
"Large animals have important parasites too: **strongyles** and **ascarids** in horses (managed with fecal egg counts and targeted deworming), **stomach worms** such as *Haemonchus contortus* in sheep and goats (checked with the FAMACHA eyelid color chart for anemia) and **liver flukes** in cattle. Overuse of dewormers has caused resistance, so testing before treating is recommended.",
sort('Internal or external parasite?',['Internal parasite','External parasite'],[['Roundworm',0],['Flea',1],['Heartworm',0],['Tick',1],['Giardia',0],['Sarcoptic mange mite',1]])]],
['Zoonoses',[
'**Zoonoses** are diseases that spread between animals and people. **Rabies** spreads through bites and saliva and is nearly always fatal once symptoms begin; vaccinating pets, avoiding wildlife and getting post-exposure treatment after bites prevent it. **Ringworm** (dermatophytosis) is a **fungal** skin infection, not a worm. **Toxoplasmosis**, carried by cats and spread through cat feces and undercooked meat, is dangerous during **pregnancy**—pregnant people should avoid cleaning litter boxes or wear gloves and wash hands. **Leptospirosis** spreads through urine; **salmonellosis** comes from reptiles, poultry and raw food.',
'Others include **cat scratch disease** (*Bartonella*), **psittacosis** from birds, **brucellosis**, **Lyme disease** (vector-borne), roundworm (**visceral larva migrans**) and hookworm larvae in skin. **One Health** recognizes that the health of people, animals and the environment is connected. Prevention: hand hygiene, gloves, parasite control, vaccination, safe food handling and prompt veterinary care.',
"Diseases are also controlled at the population level. **Quarantine** keeps new animals separate (often about 30 days for livestock) until they are healthy; **isolation** wards in clinics house contagious patients; and **reportable diseases**—such as rabies, avian influenza, tuberculosis and foot-and-mouth disease—must be reported to state or federal animal health officials so outbreaks can be contained. Shelters use vaccination on intake, cleaning protocols and careful housing to limit diseases such as parvovirus and upper respiratory infections.",
match('Zoonosis and source',[['Rabies','Saliva from bites'],['Ringworm','Fungal skin infection from contact'],['Toxoplasmosis','Cat feces and undercooked meat'],['Leptospirosis','Urine-contaminated water and soil'],['Salmonellosis','Reptiles, poultry and raw food'],['Cat scratch disease','Bartonella from scratches or bites']])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Zoonosis counseling',{a:{text:'A pregnant client asks whether she must give away her cat. (Scenario invented for practice.) What is the best advice?',choices:[['She can keep the cat; someone else should clean the litter daily, or she should wear gloves and wash her hands','b','Correct. These steps reduce toxoplasmosis risk.',true],['She must give the cat away immediately','b','Simple precautions are usually enough.',false]]},b:{text:'A puppy with bloody diarrhea and vomiting has not finished its vaccine series. What disease is a top concern?',choices:[['Canine parvovirus','c','Correct. Isolate the puppy and test.',true],['Feline leukemia','c','That is a cat disease.',false]]},c:{text:'The clinic isolates the puppy, disinfects with an effective product and explains vaccine schedules to the owner.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Diseases spread by contact, fomites, air, vectors and ingestion; colostrum gives passive immunity; puppies and kittens need a vaccine series to about 16 weeks.','Core dog vaccines: rabies, distemper, parvovirus, adenovirus; core cat vaccines: rabies and FVRCP (plus FeLV for kittens); horses: tetanus, rabies, EEE/WEE, West Nile; Coggins test; mastitis and bovine respiratory disease in cattle.','Parasites: roundworms, hookworms, tapeworms (fleas), heartworms (mosquitoes), Giardia, fleas, ticks and mites; zoonoses include rabies, ringworm, toxoplasmosis, leptospirosis and salmonellosis—One Health.'],terms:[['Zoonosis','A disease that spreads between animals and people.'],['Fomite','An object that carries infectious agents.'],['Colostrum','The first antibody-rich milk after birth.']],mcqs:[q('Which is a core vaccine for dogs?','Parvovirus','Lyme disease','Bordetella','Canine influenza','Also rabies, distemper and adenovirus.'),q('FVRCP protects cats against:','Rhinotracheitis, calicivirus and panleukopenia','Rabies only','Feline leukemia','Ringworm','Core feline vaccine.'),q('Tapeworms are often transmitted to dogs and cats by:','Swallowing fleas','Mosquito bites','Drinking clean water','Vaccines','Flea control matters.'),q('Heartworm is spread by:','Mosquitoes','Fleas','Ticks','Contaminated food','Monthly preventives.'),q('Ringworm is caused by a:','Fungus','Worm','Virus','Bacterium','Dermatophytosis.'),q('Toxoplasmosis is especially dangerous for:','Pregnant people','Healthy adult dogs','Horses','Fish','Avoid cat litter or wear gloves.'),q('The Coggins test detects:','Equine infectious anemia','Rabies','Mastitis','Heartworm','Required for horse travel.'),q('Colostrum provides:','Passive immunity','Active immunity','Vaccination','Deworming','Borrowed antibodies.')],frq:{prompt:'Design a disease-prevention plan for a new puppy, including vaccines, parasite control and steps to protect the family from zoonoses.',rubric:[['Vaccines',['series','3-4 weeks','16 weeks','rabies','distemper','parvovirus','adenovirus','noncore']],['Parasite control',['deworm','fecal','heartworm','flea','tick','preventive','monthly']],['Zoonoses',['roundworm','hookworm','ringworm','rabies','leptospirosis','hand','gloves']],['Hygiene and socialization',['avoid','unvaccinated','dog parks','clean','biosecurity','veterinarian']]],model:'The puppy should begin core vaccines—distemper, adenovirus, parvovirus (often combined as DAPP) and later rabies—at about 6 to 8 weeks of age, with boosters every 3–4 weeks until about 16 weeks, because maternal antibodies from colostrum fade at different rates and can block early vaccines. Rabies is given according to law, and noncore vaccines such as leptospirosis or Bordetella are chosen based on the puppy’s risk. Until the series is complete, the puppy should avoid dog parks and unknown dogs while still being safely socialized. For parasites, the veterinarian performs fecal flotations and deworms the puppy for roundworms and hookworms, which are common in puppies, and starts year-round heartworm, flea and tick preventives; flea control also prevents tapeworms. To protect the family from zoonoses, they should pick up feces promptly so roundworm and hookworm eggs do not contaminate the yard, wash their hands after handling the puppy or cleaning up, keep the puppy current on rabies vaccination, watch for circular hair-loss patches that could be ringworm and see the veterinarian promptly for illness. This One Health approach protects both the puppy and the people around it.'},sources:[RULES,AAHA_VAX,CAPC,CDC_PETS,CDC_RABIES]};
